The authorization server now releases a `picture` claim with the `profile` scope. A relying party cannot read this site's portrait storage, so the only way an avatar crosses between two sites is as a URL the other end can fetch; `picture` is the registered claim for it and belongs beside `name`, with nothing to opt into separately.

It is published only when a portrait is actually stored. Plone's `getPersonalPortrait` falls back to a default image, and a URL every user shares would tell a relying party that everybody uploaded the same photograph. The URL is built from the configured issuer rather than from the portal URL, for the reason the issuer is configured at all: the portal URL is whatever the request came in on, and this one is handed to another site to fetch. No issuer, no claim. @ericof
